    "description": "Let launcher find and groups take --terminal, so a caller in another terminal finds the implementer\n\n## Summary\n\nThe orchestrator can start an implementer in a different terminal from its own, for example a tmux pane while the orchestrator runs in iTerm2. Before this change, the launcher's `find` and `groups` commands looked only in the caller's own terminal, so they did not see that implementer. As a result, the orchestrator's resume read a live implementer as gone, and could open a second tab for one conversation. Now `find` and `groups` take a `--terminal` option, and the orchestrator passes the terminal that it stored at the launch.\n\nCloses #193\n\n**Ticket:** [jerridan/crew#193](https://github.com/jerridan/crew/issues/193)\n**Type:** Bug fix\n\n---\n\n## Agent context\n\n### Authority\n\n- `skills/orchestrator/references/launcher.md` owns the launcher's interface: \"Terminals\", `resume` check 4, `groups`, `find` and the exit codes.\n- `skills/orchestrator/references/orchestrator-record.md` owns `crew-orch.py resume`.\n- `skills/orchestrator/references/resume-and-close.md` owns the resume steps of an orchestrator.\n- The new finding in `docs/design.md` is §15.167, the number the orchestrator assigned. The plugin version is 0.1.135.\n\n### What changed, by file\n\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/launcher.py`: `find` and `groups` take `--terminal iterm2|tmux|herdr` and pass it to `detect_terminal`, so rule 1 of \"Terminals\" applies. With no flag, the result does not change. The output formats and exit codes do not change.\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/crew-orch.py`: `find_handles` adds `--terminal <launch.terminal>` when the item stores a terminal.\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-launcher.sh`: iTerm2 caller with tmux implementer (`find`, and `find` with no flag as the old limit), tmux caller with iTerm2 implementer, `groups --terminal tmux` from iTerm2, `find --terminal herdr` from iTerm2, a bad `--terminal` value, and `resume --terminal tmux` from iTerm2 exits 8.\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-crew-orch.sh`: an item with `launch.terminal: tmux` gets `--terminal tmux` on its `find` and resumes as `rejoin`. An item with no stored terminal gets no flag.\n- `skills/orchestrator/references/launcher.md`, `orchestrator-record.md`, `resume-and-close.md`: these files now agree with the code.\n- `docs/design.md`: the finding §15.167.\n\n### Constraints that applied\n\n- The version is not bumped, because the orchestrator owns the `version` in both manifests.\n- The README is not changed, because it does not document `launcher.py` options.\n- issue-192 changes the same launcher files (the tab command and the start check). This PR changes only `find`, `groups` and `find_handles`, and adds a test for `resume` without a code change there.\n\n### How this was verified\n\n- `bash skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-launcher.sh`: 340 of 340 checks passed.\n- `bash skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-crew-orch.sh`: all checks passed.\n- The red commit failed the new `find` and `groups` checks before the code change. The checks for a bad `--terminal` value and for `resume` exit 8 passed already, because `resume` already gave its terminal to the match. They stay as regression tests.\n\n### Known gaps\n\n- Only the seeded tests ran the change. No orchestrator ran it against a real tmux pane or iTerm2 tab.\n\n### Decisions\n\n- **A `--terminal` option, not a scan of every terminal.** A scan needs a presence check for each terminal, and an absent iTerm2 makes the `osascript` listing fail with exit 6. The item already stores its terminal, so the option costs nothing.\n- **`groups` is in scope.** It has the same gap, and the orchestrator picks a group for `start --terminal <t>`.\n- **Light path.** One package, a runnable criterion and no open preference question.\n\n### Charter\n\nThe goal: `launcher.py find` sees an implementer in the terminal that its `launch` object names, whatever terminal the caller runs in. Each caller that depends on that match gets the same result: the open-tab check of `launcher.py resume`, and the resume of an orchestrator in `crew-orch.py`. The seeded tests cover it, and the references that own these interfaces agree with the code.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
